Based on my research, here's the decision:

**Key finding:** `io.micronaut.sql:micronaut-jdbc` is the *shared abstraction* module in Micronaut SQL — it supplies `io.micronaut.jdbc.*` classes (`DataSourceResolver`, `BasicJdbcConfiguration`, `CalculatedSettings`, the `io.micronaut.jdbc.metadata.*` pool-metadata abstractions) but registers no concrete `javax.sql.DataSource` bean itself. Concrete pools live in sibling artifacts (`micronaut-jdbc-hikari`, `-tomcat`, `-dbcp`, `-ucp`), which this repo already supports — `metadata/io.micronaut.sql/micronaut-jdbc-hikari` and its test at `tests/src/io.micronaut.sql/micronaut-jdbc-hikari/7.1.2` prove the normal-usage pattern: a pool implementation + `com.h2database:h2` as an in-memory driver, wired through `ApplicationContext`, no Docker or env vars needed.

Without a pool implementation on the classpath, a scaffold depending on `micronaut-jdbc` alone can't produce a live `DataSource` bean, so the DI-driven paths through the core package (`DataSourceResolver` unwrapping, pool metadata providers) would go untested — only static utility classes would be reachable. This is the same shape as a precedent I found in `stats/org.typelevel/cats-effect-std_3/3.5.2/execution-metrics.json`: a "standard library" module that needs its runtime counterpart declared explicitly since it isn't a transitive dependency.

